  "problem": "Evaluate the expression below, expressing your answer in the form $a+b\\text{i},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers.\n\n$\\frac{12-19\\text{i}}{8-11\\text{i}}=($\n{{field-1}} \n$)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,\\text{i}$",
  "worked_solution": "The denominator is $8-11\\text{i}$ and its conjugate is $8+11\\text{i}.$ We multiply both the numerator and the denominator by $8+11\\text{i},$ so that the denominator turns into a real number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12-19\\text{i}}{8-11\\text{i}} & =\\frac{12-19\\text{i}}{8-11\\text{i}}\\cdot \\frac{8+11\\text{i}}{8+11\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(12-19\\text{i})(8+11\\text{i})}{(8-11\\text{i})(8+11\\text{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{96+132\\text{i}-152\\text{i}-209{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{8}^{2}-(11\\text{i})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{96-20\\text{i}-209(-1)}{{8}^{2}+{11}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{96-20\\text{i}+209}{64+121} \\\\  & =\\frac{305-20\\text{i}}{185} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(61-4\\text{i})}{5\\cdot 37} \\\\  & =\\frac{61-4\\text{i}}{37}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the denominator is a real number, we can write the complex number in the form $a+b\\text{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{61-4\\text{i}}{37}=(\\frac{61}{37})+(-\\frac{4}{37})\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
